  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how dams and flow regulation disrupt river connectivity in several linked ways across an entire watershed. A stylized mountain-to-lake river network shows multiple barriers and altered flow paths, with arrows indicating changes in movement through the system.\n\nThe main message is that impoundments fragment rivers longitudinally, laterally, vertically, and temporally. Along the river course, dams reduce habitat for species adapted to flowing water, trap sediment, modify downstream water transport, and block movement of animals and plant propagules both downstream and upstream. Across the river corridor, they alter river–floodplain connections: reservoirs can expand habitat for some semi-aquatic species locally, but dewatered reaches and hydropeaking disturb riparian zones, increase bank erosion, and reduce floodplain inundation important for feeding and reproduction. Vertically, the figure highlights weakened exchange between river water and groundwater, lowering habitat suitability for fish, macroinvertebrates, and wetland or riparian plants. Through time, rapid flow fluctuations appear to increase injury and mortality, alter environmental cues for migration and reproduction, and create mismatches between organism development and river conditions.\n\nOverall, the image presents dams as reshaping ecosystems by disrupting movement, habitat, hydrology, and ecological timing throughout a basin.",
